module dev.slatelang.slate

// `slate:actor` in the JavaScript back end, which is eight refusals and no implementation.
//
// **A REFUSAL RATHER THAN AN `OWED` STUB, WHICH IS `slate:lmdb`'s CASE EXACTLY.** A name on the
// `OWED` list says *not built yet*, and that is a promise somebody is waiting on. An actor here is an
// OS thread with a whole slate runtime on it -- its own machine, its own heap, its own event loop and
// its own collector -- and a JavaScript host has no such thing to give: what it has is a worker,
// which is a second *program* file reached by `postMessage` and a structured clone.
//
// **`docs/design/actors.md` has the design for the worker version and it is not built**, so the
// sentence says *the interpreter only* rather than *not yet*: a program written against `slate:actor`
// and compiled with `slate js` does not half work, and being told so at the call is the whole of what
// a reader needs.
//
// **The declaration is refused one layer up**, in `js.sysl`, where the name of the actor can be
// quoted. This is the module's own eight names, for a program that reached them some other way.
js_runtime_actor() -> string = raw"""
        // -- actors --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

        // **One sentence for all eight, and it names the one that was called.** Every name here fails
        // for exactly one reason and it is the same reason, so eight hand-written sentences would be
        // eight copies of one -- and the name is what tells a reader which line of their program it
        // was.
        const noActor = (name) => () =>
            fault("`" + name + "` is `slate:actor`, and the JavaScript back end has no actors -- an " +
                "actor is a thread with a whole slate runtime on it, which the interpreter can give " +
                "and a JavaScript host cannot. This module runs in the interpreter only")

        for (const name of ["actorSpawn", "actorSend", "actorAsk", "actorDone",
            "actorStop", "actorDetach", "actorMe", "actorTransfer"])
            B[name] = noActor(name)
    """
